  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A camera sensor does not see a finished photograph. It measures light with millions of tiny photosites. Each photosite turns incoming light into an electrical signal. Most sensors place a color filter above each site. The filters follow a mosaic of red, green, and blue, so each site records only one color, plus brightness. The processor compares neighboring sites to estimate the missing colors. This step is called demosaicing. It also adjusts brightness and reduces noise before saving the image. A photo begins as many tiny light measurements, which the processor weaves into the colors you see.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9831315122826402,
